---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.url },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Privacy",
        item: `${site.url}/privacy`,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — Close My Lid"
  description="What Close My Lid collects, and what it never touches. No account, no telemetry, no server: the app changes one documented power setting and nothing else."
  schema={schema}
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main class="prose">
    <div class="proseTop">
      <p class="proseMeta">Last updated {site.privacyUpdated}</p>
      <a href="/" class="proseBack" data-astro-prefetch>← Back to home</a>
    </div>
    <h1 class="proseTitle">Privacy</h1>

    <p>
      Close My Lid is a free, MIT-licensed app for macOS, Windows and Linux.
      It has no account, no server, and nothing to sign in to. This page explains what the
      app and this website touch, and what they never do.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>There is no account, no sign-in, and no licence check.</li>
      <li>
        The app collects no analytics, no telemetry, and no crash reports, and
        ships no third-party SDKs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing about your work leaves your computer. Your session state lives
        in local files.
      </li>
      <li>
        The only network request the app makes is an update check against this
        project's own release feed.
      </li>
      <li>
        This website has no analytics, no cookies, and no advertising trackers.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the lid is held</h2>
    <p>
      On macOS, Close My Lid holds a Mac awake with the lid shut by setting <code
        >pmset -a disablesleep 1</code
      >, and restores normal sleep with <code>pmset -a disablesleep 0</code>.
      Those two commands are the only privileged thing it does. They are
      allowlisted, with no wildcards, in a <code>sudoers</code> drop-in at
      <code>/etc/sudoers.d/close-my-lid</code> that you approve once and can
      remove at any time from Settings › Administrator Access.
    </p>
    <p>
      On Linux it takes a logind <code>handle-lid-switch</code> inhibitor over
      the system bus, which needs no root and is released the moment the app
      exits. On Windows it sets the active power plan's lid-close action to Do
      nothing for the length of the hold, saving your own setting first and
      putting it back afterwards. Neither asks for administrator rights.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app asks for no special permissions on any platform. There is no Accessibility,
      no Input Monitoring, no Screen Recording, and no keystroke watching. It
      does not read your source code, your prompts, your agent output, your
      terminal history, or your files.
    </p>

    <h2>Agent detection</h2>
    <p>
      The app shows how many sessions of each supported coding agent are
      running. To do that it reads the list of running processes — on a Mac,
      your own processes through a single <code>sysctl(KERN_PROC_UID)</code>
      call; on Windows and Linux, the process list the system exposes to any
      app. It does not spawn <code>ps</code> or <code>pgrep</code>. It matches
      executable names, and for installs that run under a JavaScript runtime it
      reads the process arguments to find the install-directory path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those counts are computed on your computer, displayed in the app, and
      thrown away. They are never stored, never logged, and never transmitted.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored on your computer</h2>
    <p>
      The app keeps your current session — the duration you picked and when it
      ends — and a few preferences, such as whether to show notifications, in
      its own folder in your user profile. On a Mac, a heartbeat file lets the
      watchdog LaunchAgent release a stranded hold if the app dies; on Windows,
      the lid setting it replaced is saved so it can be restored. Launch at
      login, when you turn it on, is a login item on macOS, a Run entry in your
      user registry on Windows, and an autostart entry on Linux. All of it
      stays on the machine.
    </p>

    <h2>Updates</h2>
    <p>
      The app checks for updates by reading this project's release feed, a file
      served from its GitHub repository. It compares the newest version there
      with its own, and if there is a newer one it offers to open the release
      page — it never downloads or installs anything itself. That request
      carries the ordinary network metadata any HTTP request carries, such as
      your IP address, which GitHub receives as the host. It does not carry an
      identifier for you, and there is no telemetry attached to it.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site runs no analytics, sets no cookies, and includes no advertising
      or tracking scripts. The Schibsted Grotesk typeface is served from this
      site rather than from Google Fonts, so loading a page makes no
      third-party request at all. Downloads are served from GitHub Releases, so
      GitHub sees those requests under its own privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      There is nothing to request, correct, or delete, because no personal data
      is collected in the first place. If you contact the project through
      <a href={site.issues}>GitHub Issues</a>, whatever you write there is
      public and governed by GitHub's privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Close My Lid is a developer tool and is not directed at children under 13.
      No information is knowingly collected from them, or from anyone else.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If the way Close My Lid handles data ever changes, this page and the date
      at the top will be updated, and the change will be noted in the release
      notes.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about privacy: open an issue on
      <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a> or reach out on
      <a href={site.twitter} rel="me noopener" target="_blank">
        {site.twitterHandle}
      </a>.
    </p>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
